Assistant: Now I can see that the AddNewTeamsForm component expects the `id` to come from URL query parameters, and it needs to be a string that can be parsed to a number. Let's modify the preview component to properly handle this:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/AddNewTeamsForm-82ce47a1.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Fix the preview component to provide the correct query context and mock necessary providers</description>
    <content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import { AddNewTeamsForm } from '../../../../packages/features/ee/organizations/components/AddNewTeamsForm';
import { FormProvider, useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
import { trpc } from '@calcom/trpc/react';

// Mock the useRouterQuery hook
import * as hookModule from '@calcom/lib/hooks/useRouterQuery';
jest.spyOn(hookModule, 'useRouterQuery').mockImplementation(() => ({
  id: '1'  // This needs to be a string as that's what comes from URL queries
}));

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    orgId: {
      type: "number",
      value: 1,
      label: "Organization ID",
    },
  });

  const form = useForm({
    defaultValues: {
      teams: [{ name: "" }],
      moveTeams: [],
    },
  });

  // Mock the trpc hooks
  const mockTeams = [
    { id: 1, name: "Team 1", slug: "team-1" },
    { id: 2, name: "Team 2", slug: "team-2" },
  ];

  const mockOrg = {
    id: state.orgId.value,
    slug: "org-slug",
    metadata: {
      requestedSlug: "org-slug"
    }
  };

  // Mock the trpc hooks as properties
  const mockedTrpc = {
    viewer: {
      teams: {
        list: {
          useQuery: () => ({ data: mockTeams, isLoading: false }),
        },
        get: {
          useQuery: () => ({ data: mockOrg, isLoading: false }),
        },
      },
      organizations: {
        listCurrent: {
          useQuery: () => ({
            data: [{
              id: state.orgId.value,
              slug: "org-slug",
              name: "Test Organization",
              members: [],
            }],
            isLoading: false,
          }),
        },
        createTeams: {
          useMutation: () => ({
            mutate: () => {},
            isPending: false,
            isSuccess: false,
          }),
        },
        publish: {
          useMutation: () => ({
            mutate: () => {},
          }),
        },
      },
    },
  };

  // Replace the global trpc object with our mocked version
  Object.assign(trpc, mockedTrpc);

  // Mock next-auth session
  const mockSession = {
    data: {
      user: {
        role: "ADMIN"
      }
    }
  };

  // Mock useSession
  const mockUseSession = () => mockSession;
  (window as any).useSession = mockUseSession;

  return (
    <FormProvider {...form}>
      <AddNewTeamsForm />
    </FormProvider>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

I've made several important changes:

1. Added mock for `useRouterQuery` to return the `id` as a string (which is what would come from URL parameters)
2. Removed the direct passing of `id` prop since the component gets it from the query
3. Added proper metadata structure to the mock org data
4. Added more complete mocks for the trpc mutations that the component uses
5. Added mock for the next-auth session
6. Fixed the structure of the mock data to match what the component expects

The error was occurring because the component expects to get the ID from URL query parameters, not as a prop. The component internally uses `useRouterQuery` to get this value. By properly mocking this hook and providing the correct data structure, the error should be resolved.

